      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says: \"the same theorem says B is not Lebesgue measurable.\" The cited target, thm-a-bernstein-set-has-inner-measure-zero-and-full-outer-measure-in-every-interval, states only that a Bernstein set has inner measure 0 and that in every nondegenerate bounded interval its intersection has full outer measure. It does not conclude nonmeasurability. That conclusion is a separate item, cor-a-bernstein-set-is-not-lebesgue-measurable. So 2.1 attributes a conclusion to the cited theorem that is not in its Statement.",
